### What the assumption ladder establishes

TS1–TS3 provide a conditional-unbiasedness guarantee. TS4–TS5 justify conventional variances, unbiased residual-variance estimation, and conditional BLUE. TS6 supplies exact finite-sample normal and null-test distributions. Strict exogeneity and no serial correlation are restrictive in many social-science time series; the lecture does not claim they hold automatically. Its next-week preparation is Chapter 11 and revision of expected value, variance, covariance, and large-sample OLS properties. The supplied Fifth Edition places Chapter 10 at printed pp. 344–379 (PDF pp. 372–407) and Chapter 11 at printed pp. 380–411 (PDF pp. 408–439), rather than the different-edition page ranges on slide p. 2. Chapter 10 supports these same explanations. Chapter 11 remains next-week reading; only passages directly needed to clarify the current assumption boundary were used, and the linked video was not opened. [[00_Materials/2026_2027/Winter_Semester/Econometrics_II/Week_2/2026_Lecture2_print.pdf#page=21|Lecture 2, PDF pp. 21, 31]]


## Worked example

O010-W1 / item_version 1, worked_example. **Prompt:** Hypothetical full-rank time-series linear model with strict exogeneity: T=20, k=2 slopes plus intercept, SSR=34, regressor SST_j=10, auxiliary R_j²=0.5; βhat_j=1.2, nullβ_j=0. Explain separately the conditional error covariance assumptions needed for conventional variance, derive the matrix and scalar formulas (show the simple-slope covariance sum), calculate σhat² and illustrative plug-in coefficient variance, and the conventional SE, t and null degrees of freedom from that same estimated variance. State the exact BLUE comparison class, what stronger error condition makes t/F exact, and what fails if serial covariance is nonzero or only marginal normality is known. Can normality restore identical regressor columns?

**Steps and interpretation:** Homoskedastic $\operatorname{Var}(u_t\mid X)=\sigma^2$ and no serial $\operatorname{Cov}(u_t,u_s\mid X)=0$ give $\Omega=\sigma^2 I_T$; they are separate restrictions. For weights $a_t=(x_t-\bar x)/S_{xx}$, Var(βhat1|X)=Σa_t²Var(u_t|X)+2Σ(t<s)a_ta_sCov(u_t,u_s|X); zero covariances then constant variance yield$\sigma^2/S_{xx}$. Matrix $A\Omega A'=\sigma^2(X'X)^{-1}$, scalarσ²/[SST_j(1−auxR_j²)]. df=20−2−1=17; σhat²=34/17=2.000000; illustrative coefficient variance=2.000000/[10×(1−0.5)]=0.400000. Conventional SE=sqrt(0.400000)=0.632456; requested t=(1.2−0)/0.632456=1.897367, exact null t_df17 only under full linear/rank/exogeneity plus errors independent of X and iidN(0,σ²); joint classical nullF also needs those conditions. BLUE is minimum conditional variance among estimators linear in y and conditionally unbiased, not all estimators. Serial covariance leaves cross terms: unbiasedness can survive strict exogeneity but conventional variance/SE/exact tests are not justified by these formulas. Marginal N(0,σ²) alone establishes neither independence across dates nor fromX. Error normality never restores full rank; exact duplicate regressor columns still make X′X singular. AuxiliaryR² is from regressing x_j on other regressors, not outcome goodness-of-fit.

## Supported completion

O010-S1 / item_version 1, supported_completion. **Prompt:** Hypothetical full-rank time-series linear model with strict exogeneity: T=30, k=3 slopes plus intercept, SSR=52, regressor SST_j=20, auxiliary R_j²=0.5; βhat_j=0.9, nullβ_j=0. Explain separately the conditional error covariance assumptions needed for conventional variance, derive the matrix and scalar formulas (show the simple-slope covariance sum), calculate σhat² and illustrative plug-in coefficient variance, and the conventional SE, t and null degrees of freedom from that same estimated variance. State the exact BLUE comparison class, what stronger error condition makes t/F exact, and what fails if serial covariance is nonzero or only marginal normality is known. Can normality restore identical regressor columns?

**Key:** Homoskedastic $\operatorname{Var}(u_t\mid X)=\sigma^2$ and no serial $\operatorname{Cov}(u_t,u_s\mid X)=0$ give $\Omega=\sigma^2 I_T$; they are separate restrictions. For weights $a_t=(x_t-\bar x)/S_{xx}$, Var(βhat1|X)=Σa_t²Var(u_t|X)+2Σ(t<s)a_ta_sCov(u_t,u_s|X); zero covariances then constant variance yield$\sigma^2/S_{xx}$. Matrix $A\Omega A'=\sigma^2(X'X)^{-1}$, scalarσ²/[SST_j(1−auxR_j²)]. df=30−3−1=26; σhat²=52/26=2.000000; illustrative coefficient variance=2.000000/[20×(1−0.5)]=0.200000. Conventional SE=sqrt(0.200000)=0.447214; requested t=(0.9−0)/0.447214=2.012461, exact null t_df26 only under full linear/rank/exogeneity plus errors independent of X and iidN(0,σ²); joint classical nullF also needs those conditions. BLUE is minimum conditional variance among estimators linear in y and conditionally unbiased, not all estimators. Serial covariance leaves cross terms: unbiasedness can survive strict exogeneity but conventional variance/SE/exact tests are not justified by these formulas. Marginal N(0,σ²) alone establishes neither independence across dates nor fromX. Error normality never restores full rank; exact duplicate regressor columns still make X′X singular. AuxiliaryR² is from regressing x_j on other regressors, not outcome goodness-of-fit.

Allowed support: show the named definition or equation in Motivation and explanation, then ask for the missing substitution and interpretation. At most two hints: first identify the relevant distinction; second point to the first keyed step. Reveal the worked solution if those fail. Supported success does not establish acquisition. Fade assistance only once the learner explains the keyed steps; then hide the solution and use an unused full check.

## Rubric and independence

Pass requires every listed component, all requested outputs and essential keyed reasoning without a critical conceptual error. Algebraically equivalent formulas, explicitly defined opposite cash-flow signs and equivalent plain-language reasoning are accepted. Partial means a missing component or a correct method with an unresolved isolated arithmetic slip; fail means the central mechanism or condition is wrong. Unscorable means the response or help/exposure conditions cannot be established. Unknown conditions never certify independence. Arithmetic slips require independent correction before a pass. No numerical grade is invented.

Critical errors: Equating variance constancy with zero serial covariance; outcomeR² used as auxiliaryR²; dividingSSR byT; BLUE among all estimators; normality guarantees rank or exact tests without independence.

Acquisition requires two distinct unassisted passes from I1/I2/D1/D2 using the exact six routes in metadata. Each full prompt and key covers every component; any pair therefore covers the whole outcome twice. A fresh D item used for acquisition cannot later certify delayed retention. Worked/support/prerequisite items and the narrow C1 correction cannot substitute for a full check. The finite bank must not be reused after exposure; request a plan_issue when fewer than two fresh full items remain.

| Component | I1 | I2 | D1 | D2 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| conditional_covariance_derivation | required | required | required | required |
| variance_df_and_null_statistic | required | required | required | required |
| blue_class_and_exact_inference_limits | required | required | required | required |

## Misconceptions and correction

When a learner drops covariance terms merely because means are zero, expand Var(a1u1+a2u2) explicitly. The cross term measures co-movement, not means. Observe the learner's reasoning before diagnosing: a sign or omitted word alone is tentative. Score before help. O010-C1 / item_version 1, narrow_correction. **Fresh prompt:** Fresh narrow check: Var(u1|X)=Var(u2|X)=2 but Cov(u1,u2|X)=1, equal weights1/2. Variance of average and whyσ²I fails? **Key and rubric:** Var((u1+u2)/2)=.25×2+.25×2+2×.25×1=1.5; Ω off-diagonal1 is nonzero, soσ²I and variance1 assumed by dropping it are wrong. Pass only if the keyed repaired component is supplied; partial for an omission or arithmetic slip; fail for the same conflicting mechanism. C1 is not a full independent check. After two ineffective hints show the relevant worked step; after another failed correction pause and return to the named prerequisite or explanation.

## Immediate and delayed checks

All items are item_version 1 and full_capability_check. I1/I2 are immediate forms; D1/D2 are distinct delayed forms. Delayed success requires an unused unassisted item before recap, at least 24 actual elapsed hours after acquisition and the most recent relevant teaching/solution exposure, with intervening exposure recorded. A date change alone does not qualify. Early D attempts may be acquisition checks only. Each item is a bounded parallel or near-context variant, not proof of broad transfer. Each key states the full required response; do not improvise extra variants.

### O010-I1

**Prompt:** Hypothetical full-rank time-series linear model with strict exogeneity: T=15, k=2 slopes plus intercept, SSR=36, regressor SST_j=12, auxiliary R_j²=0.25; βhat_j=1.5, nullβ_j=0.5. Explain separately the conditional error covariance assumptions needed for conventional variance, derive the matrix and scalar formulas (show the simple-slope covariance sum), calculate σhat² and illustrative plug-in coefficient variance, and the conventional SE, t and null degrees of freedom from that same estimated variance. State the exact BLUE comparison class, what stronger error condition makes t/F exact, and what fails if serial covariance is nonzero or only marginal normality is known. Can normality restore identical regressor columns?

**Key and essential rubric:** Homoskedastic $\operatorname{Var}(u_t\mid X)=\sigma^2$ and no serial $\operatorname{Cov}(u_t,u_s\mid X)=0$ give $\Omega=\sigma^2 I_T$; they are separate restrictions. For weights $a_t=(x_t-\bar x)/S_{xx}$, Var(βhat1|X)=Σa_t²Var(u_t|X)+2Σ(t<s)a_ta_sCov(u_t,u_s|X); zero covariances then constant variance yield$\sigma^2/S_{xx}$. Matrix $A\Omega A'=\sigma^2(X'X)^{-1}$, scalarσ²/[SST_j(1−auxR_j²)]. df=15−2−1=12; σhat²=36/12=3.000000; illustrative coefficient variance=3.000000/[12×(1−0.25)]=0.333333. Conventional SE=sqrt(0.333333)=0.577350; requested t=(1.5−0.5)/0.577350=1.732051, exact null t_df12 only under full linear/rank/exogeneity plus errors independent of X and iidN(0,σ²); joint classical nullF also needs those conditions. BLUE is minimum conditional variance among estimators linear in y and conditionally unbiased, not all estimators. Serial covariance leaves cross terms: unbiasedness can survive strict exogeneity but conventional variance/SE/exact tests are not justified by these formulas. Marginal N(0,σ²) alone establishes neither independence across dates nor fromX. Error normality never restores full rank; exact duplicate regressor columns still make X′X singular. AuxiliaryR² is from regressing x_j on other regressors, not outcome goodness-of-fit. All components are required.

### O010-I2

